<PageTitle>Retry Assurance — Execute the retry, inspect the effect</PageTitle>
<section class="opening"><span class="eyebrow">A RELEASE CHECK THAT ACTUALLY MAKES THE REQUEST</span><h1>Did a retry create<br><em>another job?</em></h1><p class="lede">Invoke your real client once. Let a pinned failure scenario commit work, withhold its acknowledgement, and expose what the internal retry really did.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/projects/new">Start a real execution</a><a class="button" href="/runner">Run the working C# examples</a></div><p class="quiet">No enterprise SDK platform required. Your code runs locally or in your own build environment. Only explicitly synthetic requests belong here.</p></section>
<section class="flow-strip" aria-label="The actual execution boundary"><div><b>01</b><h2>Declare the behavior</h2><p>One logical operation. A client-owned idempotency key. Explicit allowed terminal outcomes.</p></div><div><b>02</b><h2>Execute your client</h2><p>The real SDK uses a destination-bound HTTP transport. Its internal attempts share one operation identity.</p></div><div><b>03</b><h2>Inspect durable effects</h2><p>Two server-created resources fail the invariant. A reported success cannot overwrite the resource count.</p></div></section>
<section class="split-panel"><div><h2>A timeout is not an empty server.</h2><p>The first pack creates a synthetic job and records its effect before delaying response headers for three seconds. A new key on the second attempt creates a second job; a stable key reuses the first.</p><a href="/packs">Read the exact maintained scenario →</a></div><div><h2>Keep the evidence, change the client.</h2><p>Each run pins the behavior revision, pack content hash and declared package/build labels. Repeating creates a new run, so the original failure and subsequent result can be compared.</p><a href="/projects">Open actual projects and runs →</a></div></section>
<section class="scope-callout"><h2>What the result establishes</h2><p>A server result establishes observed synthetic effects and request relationships for this one scenario. The client's final observation and package labels are declared by the runner. No universal production-correctness, independent code-attestation or guaranteed bug-discovery claim is made.</p><p>The included broken and correct clients are first-party runnable examples, not customer activity. Pricing and market demand remain unvalidated.</p></section>
